# Secrets defined here are available for reference under registry/password, env/secret, builder/secrets,
# and accessories/*/env/secret in config/deploy.yml. All secrets should be pulled from either
# password manager, ENV, or a file. DO NOT ENTER RAW CREDENTIALS HERE! This file needs to be safe for git.
#
# Since the app stopped carrying encrypted credentials there is nothing left
# for this file to read off disk, so it holds no assignments at all. What it is
# now is the list of names a deployment has to supply, and where:
#
#   KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD        an access token for the image registry
#   SECRET_KEY_BASE                signs cookies and password-reset tokens
#   RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD   authenticates the Postmark inbound webhook
#   POSTMARK_SMTP_TOKEN            sends password-reset mail
#   SIFTBOX_READER_EMAIL           the reader's account, seeded at first boot
#   SIFTBOX_READER_PASSWORD
#   ANTHROPIC_API_KEY              writes the morning edition
#   HONEYBADGER_API_KEY            reports errors
#
# The raw values go in .kamal/secrets-common, which is gitignored. Kamal reads
# that file before this one and merges this one over the top, so a name given
# here would take precedence — which is why every name above is a comment.
# Restating one as a $VAR passthrough resolves against an unset shell variable
# and overwrites the real value with an empty string.
#
# Kamal refuses to deploy when a name in env.secret is in neither file, so a
# name left out of secrets-common fails loudly. A wrong value does not: see
# docs/deploying.md step 4.

# Example of extracting secrets from 1password (or another compatible pw manager)
# SECRETS=$(kamal secrets fetch --adapter 1password --account your-account --from Vault/Item KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD SECRET_KEY_BASE)
# KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=$(kamal secrets extract KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD ${SECRETS})
# SECRET_KEY_BASE=$(kamal secrets extract SECRET_KEY_BASE ${SECRETS})

